22. Validate Certificate Fingerprint
======================================================================

A good validation is to compare the certificate received from CMP with the certificate inside the generated JKS.

Inspect the certificate:

keytool -printcert -v -file Certificate.cer.txt

Or:

openssl x509 -in Certificate.cer.txt -noout -fingerprint -sha256

Inspect JKS:

keytool -list -v -keystore cacerts.jks

Compare the SHA-256 fingerprint.

They should match.

======================================================================
23. Validate Certificate and Private Key Match
======================================================================

This is useful if using the separate certificate and private-key files rather than relying only on the PFX.

Certificate:

openssl x509 -in Certificate.cer.txt -pubkey -noout | openssl pkey -pubin -outform pem | openssl sha256

Private key:

openssl pkey -in PrivateKey.key.txt -pubout -outform pem | openssl sha256

The resulting hashes should match.

If they do not match:

Certificate and Private Key do not belong together.

Do not use them.

