42. Do's
======================================================================

For future certificate creation/renewal:

- Use the approved CMP process.
- Determine CN and SANs before raising CMP.
- Include only necessary DNS names.
- Download the CMP-generated PFX securely.
- Verify the PFX contains PrivateKeyEntry.
- Check certificate validity dates.
- Check issuer.
- Check SAN entries.
- Convert PFX directly to JKS.
- Verify JKS after conversion.
- Compare fingerprints where necessary.
- Validate the deployed endpoint.
- Run functional POST requests.
- Start renewal well before expiration.
- Keep passwords and private keys protected.
- Verify server-side deployed file timestamp.
- Verify application logs after deployment.
- Keep application certificate JKS and Kafka truststore responsibilities separate.

======================================================================
43. Don'ts
======================================================================

Do not:

- Generate an application identity using an unrelated self-signed certificate unless explicitly required.
- Generate random private keys outside the approved process.
- Download a generic JKS and assume it should be used for the application.
- Reuse a Microsoft/legacy PKI JKS just because another application uses it.
- Mix the application JKS and Kafka truststore concepts.
- Assume HTTP 400/404/405 means SSL failed.
- Store passwords in Confluence.
- Paste private keys into emails, Teams or screenshots.
- Commit unprotected private keys/PFX passwords to source control.
- Ignore SAN entries.
- Ignore certificate expiration dates.
- Assume that a working endpoint automatically proves the certificate process is compliant.

